WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.710
Imagine a supervisor asking the team for input on a

2
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:07.270
new intake process. The intention is genuine. But in the

3
00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:10.010
meeting, the same three people do almost all of the

4
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.910
talking, and everyone else nods along or stays quiet.

5
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.680
I think that scene plays out in a lot of

6
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.800
meetings, often without anyone noticing it. So what does the

7
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.820
page mean by democratic or participative leadership?

8
00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:26.610
It means inviting structured input before a decision, while the

9
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.530
leader keeps the final call. Information is shared widely, different

10
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.410
views are sought on purpose, and the reasoning behind the

11
00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.230
final choice is explained. The page is clear that this

12
00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.750
does not mean voting on everything or giving up accountability.

13
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.510
It changes how authority is used, not who holds it.

14
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.960
So the leader still decides, but the decision is shaped

15
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.380
by the people who will actually carry it out. How

16
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.260
does that change the way the team feels about it?

17
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.810
Ownership. Instead of, I was told to do this, people

18
00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.430
say, we built this, and I understand why it looks

19
00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.630
the way it does. That shift from compliance to ownership

20
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.210
is the main reason this style is so effective when

21
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:03.490
it works.

22
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.320
What does a strong participative leader actually do in a

23
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.300
meeting? I imagine there is more to it than asking

24
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.580
whether anyone has thoughts.

25
00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.910
They start by naming what is fixed, such as laws,

26
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.170
budgets and deadlines, and what is genuinely open. They ask

27
00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.910
open questions and listen before sharing their own preference, so

28
00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.650
people do not simply agree with the boss. They manage

29
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.250
airtime so quieter, newer and remote colleagues can contribute.

30
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.530
And when they decide, they explain what input they considered

31
00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.370
and why they chose the path they did.

32
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.220
Where does this approach make the biggest difference? It seems

33
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.980
especially useful for changes that affect how people do their

34
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:42.500
daily work.

35
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.150
When the quality of the decision and real buy in

36
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.430
matter more than speed. Redesigning a workflow, revising standards or

37
00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.470
adopting new technology rarely works when designed in isolation. Front

38
00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.170
line staff see problems senior leaders miss. Involving them early

39
00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.550
catches flaws before launch and avoids the quiet resistance that

40
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:02.990
sinks decisions made from the top.

41
00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:07.130
It also grows future leaders, because people learn how budget,

42
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.730
policy and outcomes fit together.

43
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.160
And where does it fall short? I can imagine some

44
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.440
of these processes going on for a very long time.

45
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.670
Participation takes time and skilled facilitation. Without clear decision rights

46
00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.450
and deadlines, discussions go in circles. Leaders who confuse participation

47
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.430
with unanimity can end up with watered down decisions. It

48
00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.090
is the wrong tool in an urgent crisis. And the

49
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.770
most damaging failure is fake consultation: asking for input on

50
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.830
a decision already made.

51
00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:39.110
People recognize it immediately, and stop offering ideas at all.

52
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.600
The page says participation is a foundation for inclusion but

53
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.500
not a guarantee of equity. That is an interesting distinction.

54
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:47.520
Can you unpack it?

55
00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.650
On the helpful side, co designing hiring criteria, accessible communication

56
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.710
standards or culturally responsive service practices with front line staff

57
00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:00.510
and community partners makes them more realistic and lasting. It

58
00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.250
also gives diverse staff a chance to flag barriers before

59
00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:04.270
a policy is locked in.

60
00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.970
But how the process is run decides whether it widens

61
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.810
voice or quietly repeats existing power.

62
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.380
How can a fair sounding process repeat existing power? I

63
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.280
think that is the part most people would not see

64
00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:17.600
coming.

65
00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:22.430
In two ways. First, majority vote can outvote the people

66
00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.790
most affected. Deciding by vote whether to keep a workflow

67
00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.330
that is convenient for most staff but unusable for colleagues

68
00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.290
who rely on screen readers is a misuse of participation.

69
00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.290
Second, an open floor is rarely equal.

70
00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:41.330
Senior, louder and more outgoing voices dominate, while others stay

71
00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:45.310
silent, and the leader mistakes silence for agreement. There is

72
00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.790
also a hidden cost when the same few colleagues are

73
00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.570
asked again and again to advise on equity without any

74
00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:51.590
reduction in their workload.

75
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.520
So, back to the intake meeting with three people talking.

76
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.660
Calling on the quiet people by name might feel helpful,

77
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.660
but it might also put them on the spot.

78
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:06.270
Exactly. A stronger response offers more ways to contribute: a

79
00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.810
quick written round before discussion, small pairs, and a way

80
00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.110
to add ideas afterward. Then the leader explains how the

81
00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:16.870
decision will be made. Quieter voices and different communication styles

82
00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.310
are heard, and trust holds.

83
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.080
That is a very practical change that almost anyone could

84
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.380
make in their very next meeting. What else does the

85
00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:24.980
page suggest?

86
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:30.310
Keep accessibility, civil rights and anti bias requirements off the

87
00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.490
voting table, and use participation to decide how to meet

88
00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.510
them. Offer written, anonymous and structured ways to contribute alongside

89
00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:41.510
live discussion. And when people give specialized equity or lived

90
00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:45.970
experience advice, recognize it formally, with time, credit or compensation.

91
00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.640
Here is a question to carry with you. Whose ideas

92
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.520
tend to shape your decisions, and whose rarely reach you

93
00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.220
at all? Thank you for listening.
